<div data-view="story">
  <!-- opening: the tee, named, with the four ways set out as a contents list -->
  <section class="st-open">
    <div class="st-open-plate ph dark"></div>
    <div class="wrap st-open-in">
      <div class="lab" style="color:rgba(255,255,255,.72)">The Renaissance Tee</div>
      <h1 class="st-title">One&nbsp;Tee,<br>Four&nbsp;Ways.</h1>
      <p class="st-lede">One 8.2 oz garment-dyed body, cut boxy and long enough to hold a tuck.
        Four ways people in this city actually wear it &mdash; photographed the way they wear it,
        not the way a studio would style it.</p>
      <ol class="st-toc">
        <li><a href="#way-1"><b>01</b> Worn open</a></li>
        <li><a href="#way-2"><b>02</b> Layered</a></li>
        <li><a href="#way-3"><b>03</b> Tucked</a></li>
        <li><a href="#way-4"><b>04</b> Oversized</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- the specification, before the story: what the garment actually is -->
  <div class="st-spec">
    <div class="wrap">
      <dl>
        <div><dt>Weight</dt><dd>8.2 oz</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Cotton</dt><dd>Garment-dyed</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Body</dt><dd>Boxy, set shoulder</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Sizes</dt><dd>XS &ndash; XXL</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Made</dt><dd>Metro Detroit</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- the four ways: the picture sticks, the writing moves past it -->
  <section class="st-ways wrap" data-ways>
    <div class="st-stage">
      <div class="st-stack">
        <span class="ph st-shot on" data-way-shot="1"></span>
        <span class="ph alt st-shot" data-way-shot="2"></span>
        <span class="ph dark st-shot" data-way-shot="3"></span>
        <span class="ph st-shot" data-way-shot="4"></span>
        <span class="st-count"><b data-way-n>01</b> / 04</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="st-steps">
      <article class="st-step" id="way-1" data-way="1">
        <span class="st-n">01</span>
        <h2>Worn open</h2>
        <p>Straight out of the bag, nothing done to it. The collar is ribbed and double-stitched
          so it keeps its round after the first wash instead of going wavy at the back of the neck.</p>
        <p class="st-note">Shown in Bone, size L on a 6'1" frame.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="st-step" id="way-2" data-way="2">
        <span class="st-n">02</span>
        <h2>Layered</h2>
        <p>Under a jacket the boxy cut matters: no sleeve bunching at the bicep, no hem riding up
          past the waistband when you reach. The shoulder seam sits where your shoulder ends.</p>
        <p class="st-note">Shown in Washed Black under the Foundry overshirt.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="st-step" id="way-3" data-way="3">
        <span class="st-n">03</span>
        <h2>Tucked</h2>
        <p>Cut two inches longer than a standard body so a tuck holds through a full day
          rather than creeping out by lunch. The side seam is flat, so nothing bulks at the belt.</p>
        <p class="st-note">Shown in Bone with the Standard-Issue cargo.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="st-step" id="way-4" data-way="4">
        <span class="st-n">04</span>
        <h2>Oversized</h2>
        <p>Size up once and the tee reads as outerwear without losing its shape at the hem.
          The heavier weight is what makes this work &mdash; a lighter knit would collapse.</p>
        <p class="st-note">Shown in Chalk, size XXL on the same 6'1" frame.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- why heavyweight, said once, in the brand's own voice -->
  <section class="st-quote">
    <div class="wrap">
      <blockquote>&ldquo;A tee should outlast the season you bought it in. Everything else about
